== Mock Examination 2 — Complete Solutions

#sol-item(1, "B")[
  *Chapter 1.* The alga photosynthesises and supplies sugar; the fungus supplies water, minerals and shelter.
]

#sol-item(2, "D")[
  *Chapter 2.* Tissues combine to form an organ (e.g. the stomach: muscular, glandular, nervous and epithelial tissues).
]

#sol-item(3, "A")[
  *Chapter 3.* Meiosis in the anther produces haploid microspores that become pollen grains; the ovule does the same for the female gamete.
]

#sol-item(4, "C")[
  *Chapter 4.* A continuous air space system links the stomata to every mesophyll cell by a short gas pathway.
]

#sol-item(5, "C")[
  *Chapter 5.* Vitamin A is needed to make rhodopsin in the retina; a shortage impairs vision in dim light.
]

#sol-item(6, "D")[
  *Chapter 6.* Root hair cell sap is more concentrated than the soil solution, so water moves in across the partially permeable membrane by osmosis.
]

#sol-item(7, "A")[
  *Chapter 7.* Tissue fluid that enters lymph vessels carries white cells and fat, but not red cells.
]

#sol-item(8, "B")[
  *Chapter 8.* Carbon dioxide is a respiratory waste and leaves the body across the respiratory surface.
]

#sol-item(9, "B")[
  *Chapter 9.* Deamination in the liver strips the amino group from amino acids, producing ammonia, which is converted to urea.
]

#sol-item(10, "C")[
  *Chapter 10.* Enzymes are made by glands such as the pancreas; the skeleton supports, protects, moves and stores minerals.
]

#sol-item(11, "D")[
  *Chapter 1.* Cholera is caused by the bacterium *Vibrio cholerae*; the other three are viral.
]

#sol-item(12, "A")[
  *Chapter 2.* This is the endosymbiont idea: mitochondria and chloroplasts retain bacterial-style DNA and ribosomes.
]

#sol-item(13, "C")[
  *Chapter 3.* Loss of control of mitosis produces a mass that grows at the expense of normal tissue; malignant tumours also spread.
]

#sol-item(14, "D")[
  *Chapter 4.* Once the curve is flat another factor — usually carbon dioxide or temperature — has become limiting.
]

#sol-item(15, "A")[
  *Chapter 5.* Cellulose is not digested by human enzymes; its bulk gives the gut muscles something to push against.
]

#sol-item(16, "B")[
  *Chapter 6.* Guard cells gain or lose water, changing their turgor and therefore the width of the pore between them.
]

#sol-item(17, "B")[
  *Chapter 7.* Two circuits — pulmonary and systemic — are served by one pump; blood returns to the heart between them.
]

#sol-item(18, "C")[
  *Chapter 8.* About 21 % oxygen and 0.04 % carbon dioxide come in; about 16 % oxygen and 4 % carbon dioxide go out.
]

#sol-item(19, "A")[
  *Chapter 9.* The kidney removes urea and excess water and salts; glucose and cells are retained.
]

#sol-item(20, "D")[
  *Chapter 10.* A baby has around 300 bones; many fuse during growth, leaving 206 in the adult.
]

#sol-item(21, "B")[
  *Chapter 1.* Class contains orders, orders contain families, families contain species — so class is the largest rank listed.
]

#sol-item(22, "A")[
  *Chapter 2.* The external solution is more concentrated than the cell sap, so water leaves by osmosis and the protoplast pulls away from the wall.
]

#sol-item(23, "D")[
  *Chapter 3.* Clones of a well-adapted parent colonise ground fast — with no wait for a pollinator or a mate.
]

#sol-item(24, "C")[
  *Chapter 4.* The pigment is embedded in the thylakoid membranes, where the light-dependent reactions take place.
]

#sol-item(25, "B")[
  *Chapter 5.* Two incisors, one canine, two premolars and three molars on each side of each jaw gives 32 teeth in all.
]

#sol-item(26, "C")[
  *Chapter 6.* With no cytoplasm to obstruct the flow and lignified walls to prevent collapse, water moves up with little resistance.
]

#sol-item(27, "D")[
  *Chapter 7.* The aorta receives blood at the highest pressure, so it needs the thickest elastic wall to stretch and recoil.
]

#sol-item(28, "A")[
  *Chapter 8.* Chemoreceptors in the medulla respond mainly to the carbon dioxide concentration of the blood.
]

#sol-item(29, "A")[
  *Chapter 9.* The proximal tubule reabsorbs useful substances by diffusion and active transport.
]

#sol-item(30, "D")[
  *Chapter 10.* Phalanges are the finger and toe bones; carpals are wrist bones and tarsals ankle bones.
]

#sol-item(31, "B")[
  *Chapter 1.* Fungi have chitin walls, true nuclei and reproduce by spores, but they have *no chlorophyll* and so cannot photosynthesise.
]

#sol-item(32, "C")[
  *Chapter 2.* The phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins allows some substances through and restricts others.
]

#sol-item(33, "C")[
  *Chapter 3.* The way the bivalents line up at metaphase I decides which maternal and paternal chromosomes travel together.
]

#sol-item(34, "B")[
  *Chapter 4.* Glucose is used, stored or converted; it is never excreted through stomata, which lose water vapour and gases.
]

#sol-item(35, "A")[
  *Chapter 5.* Shearing teeth and powerful jaw muscles suit a diet of flesh; herbivores have flat ridged molars and a diastema.
]

#sol-item(36, "D")[
  *Chapter 6.* As the shoot transpires, water is drawn along the capillary and the air bubble moves; the distance moved per unit time is the rate.
]

#sol-item(37, "A")[
  *Chapter 7.* The universal recipient has both antigens, so antibodies against A or B are absent from the plasma.
]

#sol-item(38, "C")[
  *Chapter 8.* Glycolysis is cytoplasmic; the Krebs cycle and the electron transport chain are inside the mitochondrion.
]

#sol-item(39, "B")[
  *Chapter 9.* Secretion releases useful substances; excretion removes waste products of metabolism.
]

#sol-item(40, "D")[
  *Chapter 10.* The rounded head of the humerus fits into the socket of the scapula, allowing movement in all planes.
]

